Normal frost grows from water vapour in the air, which turns directly into spiky ice [[crystal|crystals]] on cold surfaces. Frost flowers are different. They are built from liquid water pushed out from below.

Water expands as it freezes, which is also [[@why-ice-floats|why ice floats]]. In the stem, freezing water forces the cracks wider, and the thin sheet of new ice gets shoved outwards by the water behind it. Each slit makes its own ribbon. Because the slits are slightly curved, the ribbons curl, sometimes into perfect spirals with fine lines running along them like the grain in wood.

{{story:maths-snowflakes}}

In the forest, langurs have a famous partnership with [[@deer-india|chital]], the spotted deer. Langurs feed high in the trees and drop leaves and fruit. Chital wait below and eat them.

Langurs can see a long way from the treetops. When they spot a leopard or a [[@tiger-stripes|tiger]], they give loud barking alarm calls, and the deer bolt. Chital have keen noses and also give alarm calls when they smell danger. So each animal helps the other stay alive.

{{story:ramayana}}

The most surprising experiment came in 2018 in Japan. Scientists fed bombardier beetles to toads. The toads swallowed them whole, the way they eat most insects.

Inside, the beetles fired. Many toads threw them back up, sometimes after more than an hour. Most of the thrown-up beetles walked away alive, a little slimy but fine. It is one of very few known cases of a prey animal escaping by making its predator [[@gross-vomit|vomit]].

{{story:gross-vomit}}

For about two thousand years, many thinkers followed the Greek philosopher Aristotle, who taught that moving things naturally come to rest. In the early 1600s, [[@galileo|Galileo]] tested this by rolling balls down slopes and up again. He noticed that the smoother he made the slopes, the further the balls rolled. He suggested that, with no friction at all, a ball on a flat surface would roll for ever.

[[@newton-life|Isaac Newton]] later turned this into his first law of motion: an object keeps moving at the same speed in a straight line unless a force acts on it. On Earth, friction is that force nearly every time. Far out in space there is almost nothing to rub against, so [[@voyager|Voyager 1]], launched in 1977, is still coasting away from the Sun at about 17 km every second.

{{story:voyager}}

The cloth is stretched tight on a wooden frame called an adda, low enough that workers sit cross-legged around it. Several people often work on one piece at once. They use a regular needle and an ari, a hooked needle that pulls thread up through the fabric quickly.

The design is first printed onto the cloth using a perforated paper and powder. Then the workers fill it in, often a flower, a vine or a paisley, building up layers. A heavy bridal lehenga can take a team weeks or even months.

{{story:gold-where}}

Many animals form lasting bonds. Female elephants stay with family and friends for decades, and they greet each other with [[@elephants-talk|rumbles]] and trunk touches after time apart.

{{story:elephants-talk}}

Chimpanzees groom friends and back each other up in arguments. Vampire [[@bats-fly|bats]], which can die if they go without blood for a couple of nights, share meals with hungry roost-mates. Researchers found they are more likely to help bats that once helped them.

In the 1990s, two students at Stanford University, Larry Page and Sergey Brin, built a new search engine. They wanted a name that suggested the huge amount of information on the [[@internet-how|internet]].

According to the story told by people who worked with them, a fellow student suggested a name based on "googol". When they checked whether the web address was free, someone typed "google" by mistake. They liked it and kept it. Their company is now one of the biggest in the world, and its name is a misspelling of a child's word.

{{story:languages-world}}

The Penrose stairs make a square loop. Walk up them clockwise and you climb forever, yet always return to the start. Go the other way and you walk down forever.

Each flight of steps looks normal. The trick lies in how the picture squeezes depth, so that the top step and the bottom step seem to be on the same level. Your brain accepts each bit and never notices that the whole loop is impossible.

{{story:optical-illusions}}

The [[@olympics|Olympics]] got its first official mascot in 1972. Waldi, a striped dachshund, represented the Munich Games in Germany. Since then, every Games has had one or more.

Some were animals, like Misha the bear cub at Moscow 1980. Others were stranger. The Paris 2024 mascots, the Phryges, were two red, floppy caps based on a hat worn in the French Revolution. Mascot designers try to say something about the host country in one simple shape. They also want something children will happily take home as a soft toy.

{{story:advertising}}

Traditional manja is made by hand. Cotton thread is stretched between poles, sometimes hundreds of metres long, and coated with a paste. Recipes vary, and families guard their secrets. The paste can include rice or flour glue, colours and finely crushed glass. When it dries, the thread is rough like sandpaper.

Cities such as Bareilly in Uttar Pradesh are famous for manja. When two strings cross, the cut comes from rubbing, a kind of [[@friction|friction]]. Some flyers pull in fast. Others let out lots of string. Each style has its fans.

{{story:how-kites-fly-physics}}

In his 1871 book The Descent of Man, Charles Darwin argued that the human appendix was vestigial, meaning a shrunken leftover from an ancestor. He noticed that many plant-eating animals, such as rabbits, have a large pouch at the same spot, used to digest tough leaves. Perhaps, he thought, our ancestors needed that pouch and we no longer do.

The idea of leftover body parts became a famous piece of evidence for [[@human-evolution|evolution]]. Your tailbone, a few small fused bones at the bottom of your spine, is often given as a human example: it is what remains of an ancestor's tail. Whales, for their part, carry tiny hip bones from ancestors that walked on land.

{{story:whales-walked}}

Your [[@bones|bones]] are alive. They are constantly being broken down and rebuilt, and they grow stronger where they carry weight. In space, bones in the hips, legs and spine carry almost nothing. Astronauts can lose about 1 percent of the bone in these places every month.

Muscles shrink too, especially in the legs and back. To fight this, astronauts on the [[@space-station|space station]] exercise for about two hours a day. They run on a treadmill strapped down with bungee cords, cycle and use a machine that lets them lift "weights" using air pressure.

{{story:astronaut-life}}

The story starts with a beeping metal ball. In 1957 the Soviet Union launched [[@sputnik|Sputnik]], the first artificial satellite. The United States was alarmed and set up a new agency, ARPA, to fund advanced research.

Computers then were huge, rare and expensive, and each one spoke its own language. ARPA wanted researchers across the country to share them. In the late 1960s it paid for a network to link them: ARPANET.

{{story:cold-war}}

In 1849, Abraham Lincoln, then a lawyer in Illinois, got US patent number 6,469 for a device to lift boats over sandbanks in rivers. He had seen riverboats get stuck. His idea used inflatable air chambers on the sides of a boat, pumped up to make it float higher.

It was never built for real, but a wooden model of it is kept at the Smithsonian. He is still the only US president to hold a patent. In speeches, [[@abraham-lincoln|Lincoln]] praised patents for adding "the fuel of interest to the fire of genius".

{{story:wheel-invented}}

Engineers use two main tricks. The first relies on attraction. The bottom of the train wraps around under a steel rail, and electromagnets on the train pull upwards towards it. Pull too hard and the train would clamp onto the rail, so computers adjust the magnets thousands of times a second to hold a gap of about 1 cm. Shanghai's maglev works this way.

The second relies on repulsion. Japan's system uses super-cold magnets on the train. As they rush past coils in the walls of a U-shaped guideway, they create a pushing force that lifts the train about 10 cm. This only works once the train is moving fast, so it rolls on rubber wheels until it reaches about 150 km/h, then lifts off like a plane.

{{story:magnets-how}}

Aksum, in northern Ethiopia, was one of the great powers of the ancient world from about the first century CE. Its port on the Red Sea traded ivory, gold and incense with Rome, Arabia and India. Aksum issued its own coins in gold, silver and bronze.

Its kings raised giant carved stone pillars called stelae. The tallest still standing is about 24 metres high. In the 300s CE King Ezana made Christianity the kingdom's religion, and [[@ethiopia|Ethiopia]] has one of the world's oldest Christian traditions.

{{story:salt}}

The collection looks ancient, but most of it is from after 1660. In 1649, during the English Civil War, King Charles I was executed. Parliament, led by Oliver Cromwell, ordered the royal regalia destroyed. The gold was melted down into coins. Many gems were sold.

When the monarchy returned in 1660, King Charles II needed new jewels for his coronation. Craftsmen made new crowns, sceptres and orbs, some copying the old ones. Only a few older pieces survived, including a 12th-century anointing spoon.

{{story:london-tower}}

Travel was slow and dangerous. Some forty-niners [[@usa-oregon-trail|crossed the continent by wagon]], a trip of four to six months. Others sailed from New York all the way round South America, about 29,000 km. Some took a shortcut across the [[@panama-canal|jungles of Panama]], risking malaria.

Thousands came from across the Pacific too. By 1852, about 20,000 Chinese people arrived in a single year. They often faced unfair treatment, including a special tax on foreign miners.

{{story:jeans}}

Vishnuvardhana began the Chennakeshava temple at Belur in 1117, to celebrate a victory over the [[@cholas|Cholas]]. It took about a century to complete, with his grandson finishing it.

Around the outer walls run bands of carving, one above another: elephants at the bottom for strength, then lions, horses, scrolling plants and scenes from the epics. Leaning out from the top of the pillars are about 40 bracket figures called *madanikas*, carved women dancing, playing music or doing their hair. The best known, Darpana Sundari, looks into a hand mirror. Her necklaces, bangles and the folds of her clothes are carved in such detail that they look as if they could be lifted off.

{{story:symmetry}}

In 1985 the American chip company Texas Instruments opened a centre in Bengaluru, linked to the USA by satellite. It showed that work could be sent across the world over data links. Indian companies such as Infosys and Wipro grew fast. A planned industrial area called Electronic City filled with offices.

Engineers here write code for banks, airlines and phone companies across the world, and design [[@chips-silicon|computer chips]]. In the 2010s, the city also became a hub for start-ups, building apps for food delivery, travel and [[@upi-payments|phone payments]].

{{story:chips-silicon}}

Every winter, birds fly to Chilika from as far away as Siberia, Central Asia and the Himalayas, escaping the cold. Recent counts have often recorded more than 10 lakh birds in a single season, including ducks, geese, waders and [[@flamingos-pink|flamingos]].

The best place to see them is Nalabana, an island in the middle of the lake that becomes a bird sanctuary each winter. In the village of Mangalajodi, on the northern shore, some men who once hunted birds now work as guides and protect them instead, taking visitors through the reeds in small boats.

{{story:migration}}

Everything in the Forbidden City was laid out with meaning. Buildings line up along a north–south line through the middle. The most important halls face south, which in Chinese tradition was the direction of warmth and good fortune. Rooms and courtyards are arranged in matching pairs, showing balance and order.

[[@colour-meaning|Colours]] mattered too. Yellow, the emperor's colour, covers almost every roof. Red walls stood for good luck. Small statues of animals line the ridges of the roofs. The more statues, the more important the building. The Hall of Supreme Harmony, where emperors held big ceremonies, has the most, ten in all.

{{story:great-wall}}

The most famous building in the city is Hagia Sophia, finished in 537 CE under the emperor Justinian. For nearly a thousand years it was one of the largest churches in the world, with a dome that seems to float above a ring of windows.

When the Ottomans took the city, they turned it into a mosque and added tall minarets. In 1934 the Turkish government made it a museum. In 2020 it became a mosque again, while staying open to visitors. Across the square stands the Blue Mosque, named for the blue tiles inside.

{{story:silk-road}}

Rick Riordan was a middle-school English and history teacher in California and Texas. His son Haley had ADHD and dyslexia and found school hard, but he loved Greek myths. When Riordan ran out of myths to tell at bedtime, Haley asked him to make up new ones.

So Riordan invented Percy Jackson, a boy with ADHD and dyslexia. In the books, these are signs that he is a demigod. His restlessness is a fighter's battle reflexes. His dyslexia happens because his brain is wired to read ancient Greek, not English. *The Lightning Thief* came out in 2005, and four more books followed by 2009.

{{story:ancient-greece}}
